al aids  I do have notes and I speak from the  notes sometimes when I speak  extemporaneously and at other times but  I do like to work out pretty carefully  what I'm going to say before I stand up  to say it when you test a student what's  what system do you use well I tend to  use to ask people write essays and I  tend to to give them fairly broad sort  of interpretive statements to respond to  and ask them to find examples and  illustrations to support their arguments  so I don't ask specific questions very  often I don't think history is a process  of memorizing names and dates and should  not be although of course it's important  to know some names and dates I think  understanding history means learning how  to think about history and had a  Marshall the the so-called facts that  you know behind an argument and  interpretation that's what I try to  encourage students to do you have a  favorite or favorite historians that you  look to people that you either have  modeled your own writing after or if you  you would go pick up off the shelf and  read yours if you had time oh well there  are many historians whom I admire and  whom I always read when their books come  out or some of them of course sir  they're no longer living or no longer  writing I I mean I I hate to to make a  list because I at the risk of offending  the people I leave off it one historian  whom I I've always deeply admired is  Richard Hofstadter who whom I never knew  died in the early 70s but who who taught  at Columbia is as I now do and even  though his books now seemed in many ways  very dated and present interpretations  that most historians including me  largely disagree with now he III found  to be an extraordinarily event Evander  Fist Orion in the story and who was  willing to take chances  using interdisciplinary methods and  story who wrote beautifully who tried to  make his writing important not just as  scholars but also to to other readers I  can't say it modeled my career on  Richard Hofstadter and  you know I should be so lucky as to be  as a greater the story in this as he was  but he certainly has had an influence on  the way I thought I'm a great admirer of  C Vann Woodward those of course still  very much alive still writing as a  friend of mine and who's reading his  work in college his work on southern  history had a had a big impact on me and  the way I thought about history and I  think I'll stop there I mean there are  many others who I admire but I what's  next  well I'm finishing I hope in the next  month or so a book that I've been  working on that I worked on alongside  this one for four more years that I'd  like to admit about the way in which  liberals new dealers in the late 30s and  the 1940s redefined what they thought  the government should do and it is a  book about how what came after the war  to be known as New Deal liberalism took  the form it did which is in many ways  very different from the form it had in  the brilliant mid 30s when the New Deal  itself was most active so that's that's  what I'm working on at the moment  and then I'm also just starting a  biography of Henry Luce the founder and  publisher of time and life and fortune  why Henry lives well I think I mean  there are lots of reasons why I'm doing  it but what I think makes him  interesting to me is that he it's an  opportunity to look in a very precise  way at some of the intersections between  politics and culture  I think the magazines that Luce created  and that he guided so directly through  through most of his life had an enormous  impact on American culture and also were  anonymously reflective of at least some  strains of American culture Luce was  also very active in and at times  influential in politics and and the  combination of those two things made him  interesting to me I think politics and  culture have tended to be treated as  very separate areas and again anyone  watching our politics today has to be  aware that they're not separate at all  they're inextricable and so I hope in  this book to to be able to explore that  that connection our guest has been the  author of this book called the  unfinished nation a concise history of  the American people professor Alan  Brinkley is at Columbia University and  we thank you very much for joining us my  pleasure thank you
 

